ction: ____ I was walking to school, I saw a rainbow.", "explanation": "While expresses during the whole time that an ongoing action happens. The other choices express cause, condition or contrast.", "options": ["Because", "Unless", "While", "Although"], "correct_answer": "While"}, "reason": "TEMPORAL_CONJUNCTION_AMBIGUITY"}, {"n": 583, "original_id": "79f37662-70f8-45c6-a224-45f02aa10849", "original_hash": "a2772f3527a57cc59cc67aa3e9d3acd965a1ad963f80f320b65f71827cc92435", "replacement_id": "77a8c080-82c9-561d-aaaf-a1fc0cd5a6f5", "patch": {"options": ["consequently", "therefore", "nevertheless", "additionally"], "explanation": "Nevertheless introduces a contrast: the results were useful despite the experiment being small.", "correct_answer": "nevertheless"}, "reason": "MULTIPLE_VALID_ANSWERS"}, {"n": 592, "original_id": "8cf76048-df5c-43e2-97b5-f4fe8d9fc849", "original_hash": "02c06754912a67d9af533566a96edec1539030a4edd4ab4df3ff5869629539ee", "replacement_id": "201c6fe9-333d-5028-80f4-8a559d5d74f4", "patch": {"question_text": "Choose the completion: The results were inconsistent; ____ that more trials are needed.", "explanation": "It can be seen that introduces a complete that-clause. There is no comma between seen and that.", "options": ["therefore", "it can be seen", "for instance", "regarding"], "correct_answer": "it can be seen"}, "reason": "PUNCTUATION_BREAKS_KEYED_CLAUSE"}, {"n": 593, "original_id": "8d1e4d45-85f0-473b-a02d-3e5e3dea7f55", "original_hash": "d5a429b74fa3a3642ea731442b20bcb9ce6ca435a4c96edcb810b8180f4bcef5", "replacement_id": "6305a4fa-a585-5997-9b74-a67d34ebe36b", "patch": {"question_text": "Choose the completion: The evidence was weak; ____ the hypothesis may be wrong.", "explanation": "This suggests that introduces the inference in a complete clause without repeating the phrase.", "options": ["this suggests that", "such as", "another", "in other terms"], "correct_answer": "this suggests that"}, "reason": "KEY_CREATES_BROKEN_SENTENCE"}, {"n": 607, "original_id": "a9addb8d-4b17-4a66-8e12-c93c6ca1808c", "original_hash": "5ed288df508d58b2b9995b0d3d182b756b89eff041aa04b9e14405d7d6c7e8bd", "replacement_id": "2bc57260-787f-5c7d-910f-26cee76e668a", "patch": {"correct_answer": "therefore", "explanation": "Therefore introduces the resulting conclusion. It can be seen would repeat an existing phrase and make the sentence malformed.", "options": ["therefore", "it can be seen", "for instance", "regarding"]}, "reason": "KEY_CREATES_BROKEN_SENTENCE"}, {"n": 617, "original_id": "c3feaf96-c4cd-4efd-ac5a-c2c3894d1066", "original_hash": "4c17c358c75ed216f7ae72c34a578e26a25ce93a980a5239dc14f14e5571ea00", "replacement_id": "3434d405-17f0-5c03-a6de-8ddbb45bf0d4", "patch": {"question_text": "Choose the completion: The device overheated. ____ that it shut down.", "explanation": "The consequence is that completes the result statement without a duplicated clause.", "options": ["As follows", "The consequence is", "In particular", "Likewise"], "correct_answer": "The consequence is"}, "reason": "KEY_CREATES_BROKEN_SENTENCE"}, {"n": 626, "original_id": "e968f981-af97-4234-99ab-5252e80893cc", "original_hash": "3a07308bd5431bc5a6aaca0904546a93aa955f244f67257fdd1816fe999cf12d", "replacement_id": "4731f886-3299-591a-aeca-1d61410af75f", "patch": {"question_text": "Choose the completion: The increase in reaction speed was a change ____ the higher temperature.", "explanation": "Resulting from takes a noun phrase identifying the cause; it does not directly introduce a finite clause.", "options": ["resulting from", "for example", "likewise", "in conclusion"], "correct_answer": "resulting from"}, "reason": "KEY_CREATES_BROKEN_SENTENCE"}, {"n": 640, "original_id": "1fda88a9-d9e4-4a3a-89b6-b1117f74b510", "original_hash": "2ea0c9f21d243d624c5b848f76fdb16d453c577f6988ef7ddc88dab48d17ecd2", "replacement_id": "0f87f546-6b0d-5d1f-9a12-e21556bf3d8a", "patch": {"options": ["whereas", "despite", "because", "therefore"], "explanation": "Whereas directly contrasts two complete clauses. Despite requires a noun phrase rather than a finite clause.", "correct_answer": "whereas"}, "reason": "COMPETING_CONTRAST_CONNECTIVES"}, {"n": 671, "original_id": "a59a6cbc-4745-41e5-97ae-09e49c2f5a30", "original_hash": "205da1ee04b927192e9b7104df1dc95cb430a16bb25dfa1da498c51dcb9613e4", "replacement_id": "f293fe20-006e-5678-814d-a41940af3478", "patch": {"question_text": "How is a passive verb phrase formed?", "explanation": "The passive uses a form of be followed by the past participle.", "options": ["be + -ing form", "be + past participle", "have + past participle", "will + infinitive"], "correct_answer": "be + past participle"}, "reason": "ABSENT_GRAMMAR_BOX"}, {"n": 694, "original_id": "75e79254-95de-4add-aef5-d041581a64e2", "original_hash": "2961e9e6a87c5bc000d2cdecf4242cd3c4c235a26198490e2e34cb7fffb4867b", "replacement_id": "9d38ef17-4987-5ec1-9396-240f0dd4e51d", "patch": {"options": ["at", "with", "on", "in"], "explanation": "Compare with is used here to examine similarities and differences. Compare to is also accepted, so it is not used as a distractor.", "correct_answer": "with"}, "reason": "ACCEPTED_PREPOSITION_VARIANT"}, {"n": 700, "original_id": "a38c008d-af53-4415-b89b-5bc7b479a215", "original_hash": "836a9362490bf6a3ed97848e118028156084ddd6c6b5fa27f7df8a02a915f976", "replacement_id": "9ce053cd-ed32-5f5a-a0fe-84724ae7261b", "patch": {"options": ["of", "at", "in", "to"], "explanation": "Die of lack of water is an accepted expression of cause. From is also possible, so it is removed as a competing distractor.", "correct_answer": "of"}, "reason": "ACCEPTED_PREPOSITION_VARIANT"}, {"n": 702, "original_id": "b4051846-23db-4b41-83cb-2660a1bc2586", "original_hash": "5901998f0e9034b25e3a86a6b0e23abf04731a961766f6abfb387aca81c49b0b", "replacement_id": "5889ea22-88c2-56f5-82ef-9a613607787d", "patch": {"options": ["He approved with my decision.", "He approved at my decision.", "He approved of my decision.", "He approved to my decision."], "explanation": "Approve of expresses a favourable opinion. Approve something means authorise it and is also grammatical, so it is not a distractor here.", "correct_answer": "He approved of my decision."}, "reason": "ACCEPTED_PREPOSITION_VARIANT"}, {"n": 704, "original_id": "cf6f9184-1f6c-4d6d-b670-2486a81a88b9", "original_hash": "3ffd50085fe10e41a40b21fcba5570014f37ca2b7ffd860207745c1d83f694f2", "replacement_id": "a5e2c3a8-6ae9-58ae-832d-ee781539c8e6", "patch": {"options": ["of", "at", "in", "on"], "explanation": "Die of heart disease expresses a cause of death. From can also be accepted, so it is not offered as a wrong choice.", "correct_answer": "of"}, "reason": "ACCEPTED_PREPOSITION_VARIANT"}, {"n": 705, "original_id": "d1d275fa-fed9-43dd-b69f-9f5e2dc4ef3e", "original_hash": "1130f3f6e5832130769a6fcf381b91d021d790ac3470fbe0c6583b335e6db5ce", "replacement_id": "af8e0178-c779-5071-acc9-4f27dfcc495b", "patch": {"options": ["with", "on", "in", "of"], "explanation": "Compare with is used here to examine two systems. Compare to is also accepted, so it is removed as a competing choice.", "correct_answer": "with"}, "reason": "ACCEPTED_PREPOSITION_VARIANT"}, {"n": 707, "original_id": "ef61574d-6059-4a78-8034-76ecfbd136de", "original_hash": "c1d06dbc59decad99dc47b9f0c5284275280cbef99d1d1992d4b931b5c5bc983", "replacement_id": "acdb02a7-6a13-54b5-9460-663602ea3dbe", "patch": {"options": ["of", "from", "on", "at"], "explanation": "Suffer from a cold is the intended collocation. With is also used in some varieties, so it is not offered as a wrong answer.", "correct_answer": "from"}, "reason": "ACCEPTED_PREPOSITION_VARIANT"}, {"n": 710, "original_id": "f8b96e35-8817-47de-a5aa-775cfab59fa2", "original_hash": "1b849600982d9f6c52a4d9e4d995d295a93982b598369135d6f30c0c569e803a", "replacement_id": "b13f471b-619d-51d7-8c7f-749bba96bdbe", "patch": {"options": ["has suffered of", "has suffered from", "has suffered on", "has suffered at"], "explanation": "Suffer from describes experiencing an illness. With is also used in some varieties, so it is not a competing distractor.", "correct_answer": "has suffered from"}, "reason": "ACCEPTED_PREPOSITION_VARIANT"}, {"n": 733, "original_id": "a20df6d6-0d8d-4503-9228-83d4d18b3326", "original_hash": "a252b9436ac7eafff6118a0f598e611b5686e0a6e017a3e8eb9d832c4ae70b18", "replacement_id": "6cb28085-78c0-55d0-9c77-1c1f6a8b765c", "patch": {"question_text": "Use present-simple do/does for emphasis. Complete: He ____ want to help.", "explanation": "Present emphasis uses do with I/you/we/they and does with he/she/it, followed by the base verb.", "options": ["do", "does", "did", "is"], "correct_answer": "does"}, "reason": "TENSE_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 740, "original_id": "d9b6c9a9-ae2e-49b7-ab2a-0ce572f19e7d", "original_hash": "27a0cd7081fa237d3550f35f981ad2a4395db205e7f4756beb8ec964901fa2cf", "replacement_id": "1db57d37-175f-59b5-abb7-d1a5f0bea735", "patch": {"question_text": "Use present-simple do/does for emphasis. Complete: They ____ understand the rules.", "explanation": "Present emphasis uses do with I/you/we/they and does with he/she/it, followed by the base verb.", "options": ["does", "did", "do", "are"], "correct_answer": "do"}, "reason": "TENSE_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 741, "original_id": "f78f0bed-0f53-4cde-82ff-a1f4b454ab1e", "original_hash": "9888b4e4f567843af687fef41f9f04158b1b7af39f95396ea982e801651aaad9", "replacement_id": "90ae76fd-897f-5b48-be82-7f90203ba1fd", "patch": {"question_text": "Use present-simple do/does for emphasis. Complete: Sara ____ speak French well.", "explanation": "Present emphasis uses do with I/you/we/they and does with he/she/it, followed by the base verb.", "options": ["do", "did", "does", "is"], "correct_answer": "does"}, "reason": "TENSE_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 744, "original_id": "12ab0bca-76c0-1f5f-40e0-966efda8158e", "original_hash": "a9966aa05b9e0cce603f526e323df8b24af0636cb77eaa6284d625a89f3c4974", "replacement_id": "4bc41ccb-a55f-5707-8194-7b34b21c1cd2", "patch": {"question_text": "Use do/does with stop as the action verb: Where _____ the bus stop?", "explanation": "The bus is singular, so the question uses does + subject + base verb stop.", "options": ["does", "do", "is", "are"], "correct_answer": "does"}, "reason": "MULTIPLE_VALID_ANSWERS"}, {"n": 806, "original_id": "0cbb717a-43bb-432e-ac42-b67772160491", "original_hash": "c3a291a28927ce86a3e6475ba23ffa5db0fbd70978c043ab9fab47e1480b9d03", "replacement_id": "73b87ad8-06c3-5502-b868-b138e0bf23d6", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the future continuous to describe an activity in progress at the stated future time. Choose the best completion: This evening, my parents ____ guests.", "explanation": "The future continuous uses will + be + verb-ing. Other future forms can be grammatical but do not satisfy the explicit task.", "options": ["will entertain", "will be entertaining", "entertain", "will be entertain"], "correct_answer": "will be entertaining"}, "reason": "FUTURE_FORMS_COMPETE"}, {"n": 813, "original_id": "63222cc4-966f-4165-9c9f-c8e98d469948", "original_hash": "080fec38bc7aa4484301328df0fcfd4a7d2221ec17731232dce0c7b0d00d43d0", "replacement_id": "f5125425-caad-5ea9-9f39-00d5ff620dd4", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the future continuous to describe an activity in progress at the stated future time. At 10 a.m. tomorrow, we ____ an exam.", "explanation": "The future continuous uses will + be + verb-ing. Other future forms can be grammatical but do not satisfy the explicit task.", "options": ["take", "will take", "will be taking", "are taking always"], "correct_answer": "will be taking"}, "reason": "FUTURE_FORMS_COMPETE"}, {"n": 815, "original_id": "6e2949cb-256c-43ef-babf-d743b0abe48e", "original_hash": "2efa78eb5b3d4c1a54300ecf9ffaa6f2de76ce4224aa4c09eeb6809860d24ebb", "replacement_id": "2381c27f-6a34-5418-b214-5c68304ab8f1", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the future continuous to describe an activity in progress at the stated future time. Choose the best option: Don't call at 6 p.m.; I ____ dinner.", "explanation": "The future continuous uses will + be + verb-ing. Other future forms can be grammatical but do not satisfy the explicit task.", "options": ["will cook", "will be cooking", "am cooking", "cook"], "correct_answer": "will be cooking"}, "reason": "FUTURE_FORMS_COMPETE"}, {"n": 820, "original_id": "94d1687b-07d0-453d-8724-b667eff32144", "original_hash": "a9579a1c6ccddc28e72b560d6afc374420f8396a0a138c0968faacd1cb457558", "replacement_id": "9f0bb86e-ddfb-593e-a2a0-d0c269e5b293", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the future continuous to describe an activity in progress at the stated future time. Complete: This time tomorrow, she ____ on the beach.", "explanation": "The future continuous uses will + be + verb-ing. Other future forms can be grammatical but do not satisfy the explicit task.", "options": ["will lie", "will be lying", "is lying", "will be lie"], "correct_answer": "will be lying"}, "reason": "FUTURE_FORMS_COMPETE"}, {"n": 823, "original_id": "b2493c68-0023-4475-9fc6-7ff68538dbfa", "original_hash": "2e4cb8fdf1a09b41ca6709ddb5dca56306e2ad495dfff329fd9ddd173eb8d4d1", "replacement_id": "1b9ced20-12a4-5cc6-a41e-caec48054186", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the future continuous to describe an activity in progress at the stated future time. Choose the best answer: 'Can I come at 7?' 'Sorry, I ____ then.'", "explanation": "The future continuous uses will + be + verb-ing. Other future forms can be grammatical but do not satisfy the explicit task.", "options": ["will work", "will be working", "am work", "will be work"], "correct_answer": "will be working"}, "reason": "FUTURE_FORMS_COMPETE"}, {"n": 827, "original_id": "da7b1c6b-7d43-4ae4-8ae0-27dba97868cb", "original_hash": "b80d5e1935601af09d005efc7dd4b4736ef92d8ee516920db3947126cdff93cd", "replacement_id": "b80cc329-04c3-5871-86c0-f7d7417716fe", "patch": {"question_text": "Choose the contracted negative future-continuous sentence.", "explanation": "The contracted negative is will not → won’t, followed by be + verb-ing.", "options": ["She will be not working tomorrow afternoon.", "She won't be working tomorrow afternoon.", "She won't working tomorrow afternoon.", "She willn't be working tomorrow afternoon."], "correct_answer": "She won't be working tomorrow afternoon."}, "reason": "NEGATION_POSITION_VARIANT"}, {"n": 828, "original_id": "e0f70f30-c900-40ca-82e7-2cc87e0a3f72", "original_hash": "564a88d6bbd294eea97c617a6303819065ee2b2c46511e36d95b7b8c231d4ea7", "replacement_id": "154cc7c4-db6a-5ff2-a393-056214d5d70b", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the future continuous to describe an activity in progress at the stated future time. Complete: By this time next week, he ____ in Dubai.", "explanation": "The future continuous uses will + be + verb-ing. Other future forms can be grammatical but do not satisfy the explicit task.", "options": ["will stay", "will be staying", "stays", "will be stay"], "correct_answer": "will be staying"}, "reason": "FUTURE_FORMS_COMPETE"}, {"n": 863, "original_id": "32536ea7-4aed-40bc-b0d6-78b9ec9632a4", "original_hash": "d2ef83ae4c9f3e9dfac347857e6b0aba6e480062b54bf1c43533240b4e3d6579", "replacement_id": "779bcb23-423b-5512-8940-bcb972ee62d2", "patch": {"explanation": "For big, double the final g and add -est: biggest.", "options": ["beggist", "biggest", "begist", "biggist"], "correct_answer": "biggest", "image_alt_text": "Three shapes increase in size and carry the words big, bigger and the followed by a blank."}, "reason": "EXPLANATION_OR_ACCESSIBILITY_CLEANUP"}, {"n": 864, "original_id": "47568ba6-6d96-4d11-b2e2-29a39ddc93d7", "original_hash": "dda396ff25db8535fa638f58d28816827c85b3f1bc8c430f18aa1754f159d981", "replacement_id": "b814cece-cd39-516e-9741-270c67e85c47", "patch": {"explanation": "The outside arrow points into the rectangle.", "options": ["in", "inside out", "out", "around"], "correct_answer": "in", "image_alt_text": "A rectangle has one arrow outside it and another arrow inside it, pointing toward its edges.", "question_text": "Relative to the rectangle, does the arrow outside it point in or out?"}, "reason": "EXPLANATION_OR_ACCESSIBILITY_CLEANUP"}, {"n": 866, "original_id": "99dfa854-1b80-4c1d-b84f-c210c2226122", "original_hash": "7eb64455ed22caaa6aad136c02eeb5a4d1f084601c375d0e9f6b73044e3ee819", "replacement_id": "d9a65011-a350-5fe5-b2a4-fd9e46542635", "patch": {"question_text": "Complete the superlative: big, bigger, the ____.", "explanation": "For big, double the final g before adding -est: biggest.", "options": ["biggest", "biggist", "bigest", "bigist"], "correct_answer": "biggest", "image_alt_text": "Three circles increase in size, labelled big, bigger and the followed by a blank."}, "reason": "ANSWER_REVEALED_IN_STEM"}, {"n": 908, "original_id": "d038eb41-94fd-4ce0-9198-86acb7b31358", "original_hash": "d1ee25420e7a8e0ca45d13869939bbcc8c552d02c34d208c0577b27260905632", "replacement_id": "32adcfef-fabc-5821-8d63-86a64ee7cb8a", "patch": {"question_text": "A stylist cut her hair at the salon. Choose the causative sentence expressing the same meaning.", "options": ["She had her hair cut at the salon.", "She had cut her hair at the salon.", "She had her hair to cut at the salon.", "She has hair cut at the salon."], "correct_answer": "She had her hair cut at the salon.", "explanation": "Had + object + past participle means she arranged for someone else to cut her hair."}, "reason": "CAUSATIVE_MEANING_MISMATCH"}, {"n": 913, "original_id": "efefbe3d-3e9c-429e-b993-dc46801e795e", "original_hash": "60534dd79a6099e8944233b7dc1a3f14afccb434dc8f8e65ed480c2389d3d882", "replacement_id": "fbde5fa8-101a-52d8-9028-31044b949888", "patch": {"question_text": "Someone else cut my hair yesterday. Choose the causative sentence expressing this meaning.", "explanation": "Had my hair cut means that another person did the cutting. Had cut my hair describes my own completed action.", "options": ["I had my hair cut yesterday.", "I had cut my hair yesterday.", "I had my hair to cut yesterday.", "I had my hair cutting yesterday."], "correct_answer": "I had my hair cut yesterday."}, "reason": "MULTIPLE_VALID_ANSWERS"}, {"n": 929, "original_id": "8518593a-a640-435c-ad78-b7d1194b2fe1", "original_hash": "4d98df791ea348ec0d316655c707ff5f0598e0b17b3fb15db0e682493731d65a", "replacement_id": "af7bcb56-d6c4-5ecd-9db6-442afddf9172", "patch": {"question_text": "Smoking is forbidden here. Which sentence expresses that prohibition?", "explanation": "Mustn’t means prohibited. Don’t have to means not necessary and does not forbid an action.", "options": ["You mustn't smoke here.", "You don't have to smoke here.", "You have to not smoke here.", "You mustn't to smoke here."], "correct_answer": "You mustn't smoke here."}, "reason": "MODAL_MEANING_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 930, "original_id": "87c1d299-a772-427d-8b1b-f949074930d6", "original_hash": "0fa2dbd3a0654ed5ece2553b57944174903ca4a5e4b8f4050074b4de4cd0490f", "replacement_id": "43eaafcf-3eec-5093-9ad5-62b25c647781", "patch": {"question_text": "Hurrying is unnecessary, but it is not forbidden. Which sentence expresses this?", "explanation": "Don’t have to expresses a lack of necessity. Mustn’t expresses prohibition.", "options": ["You don't have to hurry; we have plenty of time.", "You mustn't hurry; we have plenty of time.", "You haven't to hurry; we have plenty of time.", "You must hurry; we have plenty of time."], "correct_answer": "You don't have to hurry; we have plenty of time."}, "reason": "MODAL_MEANING_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 936, "original_id": "cbda09ac-d504-44f5-b723-7f3ccc9d4591", "original_hash": "27c28e0e738bc4c849992419597bfc418ef7939320d7808b625b2deb90cca2e1", "replacement_id": "53ae133e-4f70-57a9-8093-08f53c350cc7", "patch": {"question_text": "Attendance is optional if she is busy, not prohibited. Which sentence expresses this?", "explanation": "Doesn’t have to expresses that she is not required to attend; mustn’t would forbid attendance.", "options": ["She doesn't have to come if she's busy.", "She mustn't come if she's busy.", "She doesn't has to come if she's busy.", "She not have to come if she's busy."], "correct_answer": "She doesn't have to come if she's busy."}, "reason": "MODAL_MEANING_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 943, "original_id": "4a265a1a-0c9d-4bf2-8b2b-bbbe0cda11a6", "original_hash": "f890c8797cdc15f5d0ccefc871727cc6b8afbfe8ef52b43afd038577384b67d7", "replacement_id": "c566e1d2-4934-59c1-b20f-6b2148778b0f", "patch": {"question_text": "Choose the usual modern plural spelling of bus.", "options": ["buss", "bus", "buses", "bussies"], "explanation": "Buses is the usual modern plural. Busses is an accepted less common spelling and has been removed as a distractor.", "correct_answer": "buses"}, "reason": "ACCEPTED_SPELLING_VARIANT"}, {"n": 995, "original_id": "d381bda7-3226-4b15-a585-ddfcbcd37db2", "original_hash": "f0c9053d993559312db07f15da59a3f21b18c63dde3dac92dbcbc93d062c34cb", "replacement_id": "a61ed7a3-3e76-5614-9853-5b449f5efa3f", "patch": {"question_text": "Which word is the subject pronoun in this sentence? They asked us to wait outside.", "explanation": "They is the subject pronoun. Us is also a pronoun, but it is an object pronoun.", "options": ["They", "asked", "us", "outside"], "correct_answer": "They"}, "reason": "MULTIPLE_VALID_ANSWERS"}, {"n": 998, "original_id": "0281f363-b593-421d-ae6b-75a1703207bc", "original_hash": "9cca6fbd364d2e02ec9b72b5f118604e993830f4e821cd55369764ea0cacd6fa", "replacement_id": "22d1a32e-4fc2-5027-96fb-3946f6b56d72", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the past continuous for the ongoing action. Choose the correct sentence: I ____ (write) an email, so I couldn't answer.", "explanation": "The ongoing past action takes was/were + verb-ing. A single event interrupting it takes the past simple.", "options": ["am writing", "was writing", "were writing", "wrote"], "correct_answer": "was writing"}, "reason": "PAST_ASPECT_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 999, "original_id": "096d7ee2-9ccf-46b7-8547-b159c01f68e1", "original_hash": "562dfd9172ee791a7bb7faa6acadab27e751a657c02bab58d0ce01180c8df716", "replacement_id": "d02f776d-e287-5091-8c60-9d781504e7f6", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the past continuous for the ongoing action. Choose the correct form: He ____ (work) late yesterday evening.", "explanation": "The ongoing past action takes was/were + verb-ing. A single event interrupting it takes the past simple.", "options": ["worked", "was working", "were working", "is working"], "correct_answer": "was working"}, "reason": "PAST_ASPECT_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 1000, "original_id": "1300faf4-7f44-491b-a92c-cb16071ea0d2", "original_hash": "af9a5f486a9049c8ac0c6a5d2120fe59cbe5c0b442abd3122651aaa6e608ce2c", "replacement_id": "0511f912-58fa-59c7-9f71-2529f5094c95", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the past continuous for the ongoing action. Choose the correct sentence: While I ____ dinner, the phone rang.", "explanation": "The ongoing past action takes was/were + verb-ing. A single event interrupting it takes the past simple.", "options": ["were cooking", "was cooking", "cooked", "am cooking"], "correct_answer": "was cooking"}, "reason": "PAST_ASPECT_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 1001, "original_id": "1c036628-3c12-4d5a-9d28-5c25a45678a6", "original_hash": "189a5c123f1434b18bfc9e03cd56d32ddd7153aca8269be196f8e73713ac2869", "replacement_id": "9b3f0a49-d807-574f-8fda-89f9588f1159", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the past continuous for the ongoing action. Choose the correct form: We ____ (talk) when the teacher came in.", "explanation": "The ongoing past action takes was/were + verb-ing. A single event interrupting it takes the past simple.", "options": ["talked", "were talking", "are talking", "was talking"], "correct_answer": "were talking"}, "reason": "PAST_ASPECT_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 1005, "original_id": "43889dd5-0c0c-4eea-95ac-0a04b509c899", "original_hash": "c5bd8a71ce900f3411bc0a5304cb5e163d4bb7aa1edb532f70dd41d06173ebeb", "replacement_id": "2c0914a6-b646-5747-90b6-88452ffda1d5", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the past continuous for the ongoing action. Choose the correct sentence.", "explanation": "The ongoing past action takes was/were + verb-ing. A single event interrupting it takes the past simple.", "options": ["At that moment, I were thinking about my future.", "At that moment, I was thinking about my future.", "At that moment, I thought about my future.", "At that moment, I was think about my future."], "correct_answer": "At that moment, I was thinking about my future."}, "reason": "PAST_ASPECT_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 1006, "original_id": "4c120436-db55-4f72-9b44-ca6fb663442e", "original_hash": "9f51d3d016223a394d7a1b5e72a7badc842985ea421d5607dc3e41d9f74575a3", "replacement_id": "7eaaa5a3-7c33-5300-9eaf-5903f5daa226", "patch": {"options": ["They were cooking dinner while they were watching a movie.", "They cooked dinner and watched a movie.", "They was cooking dinner and watched a movie.", "They were cooking dinner and they watched a movie."], "correct_answer": "They was cooking dinner and watched a movie.", "explanation": "They requires were, not was. Using different aspects in coordinated clauses is not automatically an error."}, "reason": "NO_INCORRECT_OPTION"}, {"n": 1007, "original_id": "69506eb2-dee3-4919-b612-4339012a77e8", "original_hash": "30a31a6ec35e5ce2b30126cf43b7d30130953a4ffd85d473803e0e2b61b4188c", "replacement_id": "33cef2ba-1574-55f9-a999-f3cb2edea3ab", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the past continuous for the ongoing action. Choose the best option: She ____ (not/pay) attention, so she didn't hear the instructions.", "explanation": "The ongoing past action takes was/were + verb-ing. A single event interrupting it takes the past simple.", "options": ["weren't paying", "wasn't paying", "isn't paying", "didn't pay"], "correct_answer": "wasn't paying"}, "reason": "PAST_ASPECT_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 1009, "original_id": "7622e666-17ed-401e-a83e-099e972a6083", "original_hash": "7d4614fd2c084735b4d8da14f056ecbd211d8d4d91ca2e8743f05961646fa25b", "replacement_id": "cda615d0-0c21-58cd-a73a-f194cfc6751a", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the past continuous for the ongoing action. Choose the best option: The kids ____ outside all afternoon yesterday.", "explanation": "The ongoing past action takes was/were + verb-ing. A single event interrupting it takes the past simple.", "options": ["played", "was playing", "are playing", "were playing"], "correct_answer": "were playing"}, "reason": "PAST_ASPECT_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 1010, "original_id": "7844855d-b0af-4d7c-9a8b-c271034a188b", "original_hash": "3737efff1a6d4645fa6e8f40202834ddf9a0cbfd77732d2fff3ffac4e8730930", "replacement_id": "afddef4e-e983-5dd0-a19c-192bf75dbd9c", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the past continuous for the ongoing action. Choose the best option: When I arrived, they ____ dinner.", "explanation": "The ongoing past action takes was/were + verb-ing. A single event interrupting it takes the past simple.", "options": ["were having", "had", "are having", "was having"], "correct_answer": "were having"}, "reason": "PAST_ASPECT_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 1011, "original_id": "90c9b003-23bc-4083-a450-1e9d2e1f9628", "original_hash": "d55196d94d524b526e81cd483a3f68358f5a50cc81764979743703113f5ae749", "replacement_id": "c376e613-a313-5f13-ac0f-6f98fe5aebdb", "patch": {"question_text": "Choose the sentence using the past continuous for both simultaneous activities.", "explanation": "Was listening and was doing both use the past continuous to present simultaneous ongoing activities.", "options": ["I listened to music while I was doing my homework.", "I was listening to music while I did my homework.", "I listened to music while I did my homework.", "I was listening to music while I was doing my homework."], "correct_answer": "I was listening to music while I was doing my homework."}, "reason": "MULTIPLE_VALID_ANSWERS"}, {"n": 1013, "original_id": "b9cf410e-4db1-451f-ac7e-2ffa145ff440", "original_hash": "5ef48c453477f053a3cd92fffe05266a312429b63c1d0b8fe3825c47fdf7590c", "replacement_id": "51e02ca1-b194-550c-978c-a76b15214b1c", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the past continuous for the ongoing action. Complete: She ____ (study) when I called her.", "explanation": "The ongoing past action takes was/were + verb-ing. A single event interrupting it takes the past simple.", "options": ["was studying", "were studying", "is studying", "studied"], "correct_answer": "was studying"}, "reason": "PAST_ASPECT_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 1015, "original_id": "bd46fc48-4814-4121-be8c-c289eb95cade", "original_hash": "045730063d3321120fc3877daa054936aa0b6276576db7467e7c61f53409f0ea", "replacement_id": "438e411e-aa15-525b-85e6-394c175a87a8", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the past continuous for the ongoing reading and the past simple for the single text message. Choose the correct contrast: I ____ (read) when you ____ (text) me.", "explanation": "The ongoing past action takes was/were + verb-ing. A single event interrupting it takes the past simple.", "options": ["was reading / texted", "was reading / was texting", "read / was texting", "read / texted"], "correct_answer": "was reading / texted"}, "reason": "PAST_ASPECT_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 1016, "original_id": "bd6b257a-7d66-433f-bb85-6f1c09001a83", "original_hash": "3011c0ff5a0c2fc1adaabe1d9ba46bc6faf4e10a958ff9a133181477a9e7d4d8", "replacement_id": "808e19d1-16b4-519e-8c32-bf4923a61bbd", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the past continuous for the ongoing action. Complete: It ____ (rain) when we left the house.", "explanation": "The ongoing past action takes was/were + verb-ing. A single event interrupting it takes the past simple.", "options": ["rained", "were raining", "is raining", "was raining"], "correct_answer": "was raining"}, "reason": "PAST_ASPECT_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 1018, "original_id": "cd8ffe8c-8105-494b-ad36-6a6c683191bc", "original_hash": "ea2017b608f644b4db161726b6502abf32c341944a6d548afe11dfb272155733", "replacement_id": "48099922-dd27-5d10-abff-855e5e9993fb", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the past continuous for the ongoing action. Choose the best option: At 6 p.m. yesterday, we ____ home.", "explanation": "The ongoing past action takes was/were + verb-ing. A single event interrupting it takes the past simple.", "options": ["are driving", "drove", "were driving", "was driving"], "correct_answer": "were driving"}, "reason": "PAST_ASPECT_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 1019, "original_id": "f14390d9-530e-4567-b4f6-647c603e2c34", "original_hash": "f3fab9a9f153279e5dbcedd38d35c1bb7ee12bc6b946ecb109217ca73329f3a3", "replacement_id": "5fe0189b-c350-58f3-b0f5-3bc83675cf5b", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the past continuous for the ongoing action. Choose the correct question: ____ you ____ at 10 last night?", "explanation": "The ongoing past action takes was/were + verb-ing. A single event interrupting it takes the past simple.", "options": ["Were / studying", "Was / study", "Did / study", "Were / study"], "correct_answer": "Were / studying"}, "reason": "PAST_ASPECT_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 1024, "original_id": "13321111-c5be-4570-a71d-b5d39eb0dafd", "original_hash": "aebd35e4b94204bd5f7388c6260b63eb30865cefaea1d5f191bf1610ad8e8dd8", "replacement_id": "40059c16-9e50-5ab4-adde-c625b61bc0dd", "patch": {"options": ["It was forbidden to pay.", "It was necessary to pay.", "It was not necessary, but you paid anyway.", "Payment was not necessary; the sentence alone does not say whether you paid."], "correct_answer": "Payment was not necessary; the sentence alone does not say whether you paid.", "explanation": "Didn’t need to means there was no necessity. It does not by itself establish whether the person paid."}, "reason": "MEANING_OVERCLAIM"}, {"n": 1031, "original_id": "696610c2-b853-4858-9eed-98ce82f6090c", "original_hash": "c0ea746da1aad99a451e5cc5795dd5811fb28b202a8dd4a3779bf1f9927a6472", "replacement_id": "30f57185-b0b4-5d48-bb9c-52ff9c9af901", "patch": {"question_text": "Use a past modal expressing uncertainty, not a definite deduction: He ____ (not/see) the sign; he drove too fast.", "explanation": "Might not have seen expresses an uncertain possibility about the past.", "options": ["might not have seen", "might not see", "must not have seen", "can't have seen"], "correct_answer": "might not have seen"}, "reason": "MODAL_INTERPRETATION_COMPETES"}, {"n": 1039, "original_id": "91b8697c-d62c-42dc-9a42-f0c5fcb94b8b", "original_hash": "41c6cc6bef13e14e858697fe379816df4ac3eb560f4d254e9339d7b4058cb372", "replacement_id": "cc823193-8942-5540-bd3f-4a66afe80e68", "patch": {"question_text": "Choose the sentence expressing criticism of something someone already said.", "explanation": "Shouldn’t have + past participle criticises a completed past action.", "options": ["You shouldn't have said that.", "You shouldn't said that.", "You mustn't have said that.", "You shouldn't have say that."], "correct_answer": "You shouldn't have said that."}, "reason": "MODAL_INTERPRETATION_COMPETES"}, {"n": 1044, "original_id": "f947501e-4e52-4644-b048-cd66de1938fb", "original_hash": "dddce74042c9fe4c1ab1c9a242271a9d2a84917384f5fd88b0a721104c481974", "replacement_id": "12fb45b9-5933-5d14-917d-a574b8920ec8", "patch": {"question_text": "He rushed, but it was unnecessary because he had plenty of time. Complete with a past modal of unnecessary action: He ____ rushed.", "explanation": "Needn’t have + past participle means the action happened but was not necessary.", "options": ["shouldn't have", "mustn't have", "needn't have", "didn't need to"], "correct_answer": "needn't have"}, "reason": "MODAL_INTERPRETATION_COMPETES"}, {"n": 1045, "original_id": "fa5813cd-90bc-49ae-a43c-7c5c0ab86d62", "original_hash": "ac3680a18ba7a88312dc6cbf355d1eb236d966b3fe085921b3644617db3b8077", "replacement_id": "79e9798f-e37a-59ef-bacf-df6e29c25ccd", "patch": {"question_text": "It is impossible that he studied enough: he did no revision at all. He ____ studied enough.", "explanation": "Can’t have expresses impossibility about a past action. Failure alone would not prove lack of study.", "options": ["should have", "can't have", "could have", "must have"], "correct_answer": "can't have"}, "reason": "UNJUSTIFIED_DEDUCTION"}, {"n": 1057, "original_id": "6ef7a49d-bb44-4cc1-9ef1-a05046b64bf8", "original_hash": "434c618ad6a653963ec0b91d1dc24fe0788da963171846c8ebed1956c6ed4cc0", "replacement_id": "210ecdbe-05c4-57c0-b2ba-22a2403ed483", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the past perfect to mark the action completed before the other past event. Choose the best option: After he ____ dinner, he went for a walk.", "explanation": "The task requests had + past participle, or had + been + past participle in the passive, to make the earlier past action explicit.", "options": ["ate", "had eaten", "had eat", "has eaten"], "correct_answer": "had eaten"}, "reason": "PAST_PERFECT_NOT_REQUIRED"}, {"n": 1060, "original_id": "7e7b1074-2fdb-4add-b6e0-023e67f9b7f9", "original_hash": "7b7084fc6e03614b6491f2cfb8047a526a33a31715eb4b9af94984c542b38782", "replacement_id": "d1f33f6f-f317-5be5-985a-dd2c75975465", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the past perfect to mark the action completed before the other past event. Choose the best option: He couldn't get in because the door ____.", "explanation": "The task requests had + past participle, or had + been + past participle in the passive, to make the earlier past action explicit.", "options": ["had been locked", "has been locked", "had locked", "was locked"], "correct_answer": "had been locked"}, "reason": "PAST_PERFECT_NOT_REQUIRED"}, {"n": 1065, "original_id": "c564cc51-6f49-47eb-aeff-b9f2c3418874", "original_hash": "162a46a970fce145d9275953bcdabc4dea3276e47ce500939f479416e15cf513", "replacement_id": "a301b41a-bc1b-5be6-92ea-dc7eccf458d4", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the past perfect to mark the action completed before the other past event. Choose the correct sentence.", "explanation": "The task requests had + past participle, or had + been + past participle in the passive, to make the earlier past action explicit.", "options": ["She was upset because her phone was lost.", "She was upset because her phone had been lost.", "She was upset because her phone has been lost.", "She was upset because her phone had lost."], "correct_answer": "She was upset because her phone had been lost."}, "reason": "PAST_PERFECT_NOT_REQUIRED"}, {"n": 1128, "original_id": "eef71327-d1f8-4c76-b725-5c516a300043", "original_hash": "d9a898ba3bab3956276513638fc8c24bbbc5e013665a26e8b43e5eb5d75f6475", "replacement_id": "dab8ff58-d2f5-52e9-8af2-e5ae4dcbfa3a", "patch": {"question_text": "Choose the present-perfect passive sentence focusing on the bridge receiving the action.", "explanation": "Has been opened is present perfect passive: has + been + past participle.", "options": ["Someone has opened a new bridge.", "A new bridge has opened.", "A new bridge has been opened.", "A new bridge opened."], "correct_answer": "A new bridge has been opened."}, "reason": "MULTIPLE_VALID_NEWS_FORMS"}, {"n": 1140, "original_id": "a5cf5c0e-f5d4-42af-be5e-3ff7033c1c73", "original_hash": "0573b187f4dbb86abf39f01ce92e2562ee6f4a2782c3117099c0c4c4d5ec6c04", "replacement_id": "f5390396-0d9f-5f2d-87e7-14018665ae0d", "patch": {"question_text": "Complete in the present perfect active. Choose the correct sentence: (Active) I ____ my keys.", "explanation": "With I, the present perfect active uses have + past participle: have lost.", "options": ["have lost", "has lost", "have been lost", "lost"], "correct_answer": "have lost"}, "reason": "TENSE_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 1150, "original_id": "f98ac0d5-3636-47bf-b7e7-a468df7a79ae", "original_hash": "2807e22f732634acdb03c59d84839e26d90f4b8e662eb608db02e1e7d0242ae4", "replacement_id": "6ad40210-c515-5b7a-ab3e-ab3f25829a04", "patch": {"question_text": "Which sentence is not in the present perfect passive?", "explanation": "Has cooked is active. The other choices use has been + past participle. The active sentence can be grammatical.", "options": ["The food has been cooked.", "The food has cooked.", "The food has been prepared.", "The food has been eaten."], "correct_answer": "The food has cooked."}, "reason": "GRAMMATICAL_KEY_MARKED_WRONG"}, {"n": 1153, "original_id": "1012aa6e-273e-4a44-9c4f-52de75128138", "original_hash": "8d103c35133b917cf4d9e59720694b55c925c4452541971fe6cb38e2d06f7556", "replacement_id": "960f113d-4b45-5b48-8890-1ea39f2c40ed", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the present perfect continuous to emphasize the duration or repeated activity. Choose the best option: I ____ too much coffee lately.", "explanation": "The requested present perfect continuous uses have/has + been + verb-ing. The present perfect simple may be grammatical in other contexts, but is not the form requested here.", "options": ["drink", "have drunk", "have been drinking", "drank"], "correct_answer": "have been drinking"}, "reason": "PERFECT_ASPECT_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 1154, "original_id": "241e8f10-8c0d-4c98-81e4-a46a96980cbf", "original_hash": "b61321480398e8580aa4858a40ef7994a84946b096df615290076fa81255b260", "replacement_id": "fb7fb924-3b75-5c46-bd10-87cf87ff5b1a", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the present perfect continuous to emphasize the duration or repeated activity. Choose the best option: It ____ all day, so the streets are wet.", "explanation": "The requested present perfect continuous uses have/has + been + verb-ing. The present perfect simple may be grammatical in other contexts, but is not the form requested here.", "options": ["rained", "has rained", "has been raining", "is raining"], "correct_answer": "has been raining"}, "reason": "PERFECT_ASPECT_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 1155, "original_id": "27c9919c-a214-439d-a483-04ddb5842b05", "original_hash": "883feff07b4ea6b7534fbd983829aac72c4b7d9c0dc11b3dfe22195c1993153a", "replacement_id": "09f56716-9b0c-5d74-b51c-96b2b117b973", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the present perfect continuous to emphasize the duration or repeated activity. Choose the best option: The kids ____ outside for hours, but now they’re inside.", "explanation": "The requested present perfect continuous uses have/has + been + verb-ing. The present perfect simple may be grammatical in other contexts, but is not the form requested here.", "options": ["played", "have played", "have been playing", "are playing"], "correct_answer": "have been playing"}, "reason": "PERFECT_ASPECT_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 1158, "original_id": "4d76c0ce-62e3-42ea-9050-2bcbadaba383", "original_hash": "a20c963f01562e483c85a6e2b878d88f842c65aeed7be01c18d17196cdac63e0", "replacement_id": "4229c1c8-c8c3-552a-8ad3-5be35231c720", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the present perfect continuous to emphasize the duration or repeated activity. Choose the best option: I ____ for you since 2 o’clock.", "explanation": "The requested present perfect continuous uses have/has + been + verb-ing. The present perfect simple may be grammatical in other contexts, but is not the form requested here.", "options": ["wait", "have waited", "have been waiting", "am waited"], "correct_answer": "have been waiting"}, "reason": "PERFECT_ASPECT_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 1160, "original_id": "5cd16299-ecc3-43d9-be33-7e43fa3c7c80", "original_hash": "fb92539ca650c759073550f3c6acb51ecd994e485b684b34d734bafcfb0df1ea", "replacement_id": "20148ee9-9f34-5fcd-b68f-3d6a3869a2bc", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the present perfect continuous to emphasize the duration or repeated activity. Choose the correct form: She ____ on this project since Monday.", "explanation": "The requested present perfect continuous uses have/has + been + verb-ing. The present perfect simple may be grammatical in other contexts, but is not the form requested here.", "options": ["has been working", "is working", "worked", "has worked"], "correct_answer": "has been working"}, "reason": "PERFECT_ASPECT_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 1162, "original_id": "71c38c32-4f56-4193-b651-5027fa33b9c6", "original_hash": "6284d9e432cc19b327efb31f9770acb5db8814007322123b08