ng to", "listened to"], "correct_answer": "have been listening to"}, "reason": "PERFECT_ASPECT_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 1166, "original_id": "bb5da6b4-c7a6-47b0-8263-9b8884a1f86e", "original_hash": "7b7aa16587fa186cefa04d9b6a5dbbe530322aeedae3d11ad94316812084f199", "replacement_id": "f8350bd1-e0ea-5a14-8f34-6f64620d93b4", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the present perfect continuous to emphasize the duration or repeated activity. Choose the correct form: How long ____ you ____ here?", "explanation": "The requested present perfect continuous uses have/has + been + verb-ing. The present perfect simple may be grammatical in other contexts, but is not the form requested here.", "options": ["do / live", "have / been living", "did / live", "are / living"], "correct_answer": "have / been living"}, "reason": "PERFECT_ASPECT_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 1167, "original_id": "bc3894a3-cc53-4d1c-9323-8c5b0d7865fc", "original_hash": "bffccda469a1a8d1ceae48bafb37d4b8fbcbe33db34b9e81d87eade613463429", "replacement_id": "4ff9b9ff-681b-5f3c-8728-b6bcbf2f195a", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the present perfect continuous to emphasize the duration or repeated activity. Choose the best option: I ____ all afternoon, and I’m exhausted.", "explanation": "The requested present perfect continuous uses have/has + been + verb-ing. The present perfect simple may be grammatical in other contexts, but is not the form requested here.", "options": ["have studied", "have been studying", "study", "studied"], "correct_answer": "have been studying"}, "reason": "PERFECT_ASPECT_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 1173, "original_id": "1f0e21b3-9e16-46e7-9e71-ae82b8cc2daa", "original_hash": "b02639be3278fe3c46f841c02d098aadae0b13bbf40a2bef96fb224be547bc5e", "replacement_id": "b2cca9d7-a1fd-5160-8856-e029740c1849", "patch": {"question_text": "Complete in the present simple passive: Coffee ___ in many countries around the world.", "explanation": "Coffee is singular/uncountable, so the present simple passive uses is + grown.", "options": ["grow", "is grown", "are grown", "grows"], "correct_answer": "is grown"}, "reason": "MULTIPLE_VALID_ANSWERS"}, {"n": 1174, "original_id": "5b8fccda-fd5d-40ad-9416-7bf2be896e42", "original_hash": "73ce0544b990a65261b8cbe55d56c6da61f8fa7955b2285e0f735587cf0390e8", "replacement_id": "6ae83b25-7e22-5d14-ac12-c09cabe0041f", "patch": {"explanation": "The subject receives the action. The present simple passive uses am/is/are + past participle; plural subjects take are, including aren’t in negative statements.", "options": ["checks", "is checked", "are checked", "checked"], "correct_answer": "are checked"}, "reason": "EXPLANATION_OR_ACCESSIBILITY_CLEANUP"}, {"n": 1175, "original_id": "73e836ae-5fd2-4d70-af80-d07e26ef46ae", "original_hash": "6e544d55eef6c439c6d3bfcb7211d7620fad9080fb8c4cf13df771a07e5b6536", "replacement_id": "8c13c132-a19d-571a-9e63-85dfe66a4637", "patch": {"explanation": "The subject receives the action. The present simple passive uses am/is/are + past participle; plural subjects take are, including aren’t in negative statements.", "options": ["is used", "uses", "used", "are used"], "correct_answer": "is used"}, "reason": "EXPLANATION_OR_ACCESSIBILITY_CLEANUP"}, {"n": 1176, "original_id": "7cbc0ae2-3c68-4cb6-bdf8-b014890ff0c6", "original_hash": "7cd92709cbabb47e9e099774ea63844c6d77b2e205f3037f78c51a65e6d9413c", "replacement_id": "3169f09b-1a1f-5def-a587-20b69c21c536", "patch": {"explanation": "The subject receives the action. The present simple passive uses am/is/are + past participle; plural subjects take are, including aren’t in negative statements.", "options": ["announces", "are announced", "is announced", "announced"], "correct_answer": "are announced"}, "reason": "EXPLANATION_OR_ACCESSIBILITY_CLEANUP"}, {"n": 1177, "original_id": "7d14df2a-05f4-4c20-9e49-d46a0d36ec51", "original_hash": "555cb17edaed01d78cf9b1eb0a707c0dc548b3fe119f310ab4873f91be0d7d74", "replacement_id": "4cb69265-1a32-53c9-a1c1-657173b8becf", "patch": {"question_text": "Complete in the present simple passive: The classrooms ___ after the last lesson every day.", "correct_answer": "are cleaned", "explanation": "The plural classrooms receives the cleaning action: are + past participle cleaned. Are clean describes a state, not the passive verb form.", "options": ["clean", "are clean", "are cleaned", "is cleaned"]}, "reason": "INCORRECT_PASSIVE_KEY"}, {"n": 1178, "original_id": "a4f98580-2a76-432a-ab65-0ed39da68c6e", "original_hash": "380aba0ad05191ab64a2274ea1f5eef4c185c112a56dedb100bb76b5bb74b1f9", "replacement_id": "2390e9bd-6887-50ea-bc24-960fed1ee689", "patch": {"explanation": "The subject receives the action. The present simple passive uses am/is/are + past participle; plural subjects take are, including aren’t in negative statements.", "options": ["aren’t allowed", "isn’t allowed", "don’t allowed", "not allowed"], "correct_answer": "aren’t allowed"}, "reason": "EXPLANATION_OR_ACCESSIBILITY_CLEANUP"}, {"n": 1179, "original_id": "ad201af2-fabf-4620-8fa9-0be3385a88f9", "original_hash": "b731d749c3225ce32c548f4cb291f3063e1a5aade19cda00cba669ccf3f20ed7", "replacement_id": "e7702c6e-cfa2-5a02-acfa-8c16b9a974c8", "patch": {"explanation": "The subject receives the action. The present simple passive uses am/is/are + past participle; plural subjects take are, including aren’t in negative statements.", "options": ["is / make", "does / made", "is / made", "are / made"], "correct_answer": "is / made"}, "reason": "EXPLANATION_OR_ACCESSIBILITY_CLEANUP"}, {"n": 1180, "original_id": "b38ae990-3a5d-4f2c-a40b-0878da571cb9", "original_hash": "4f7d623c563a59af51d70d857c305303797e0c4b382c2fbc25facd7136ccfc66", "replacement_id": "fb69e868-469d-5757-8a0a-bc06e4ba4ba9", "patch": {"explanation": "The subject receives the action. The present simple passive uses am/is/are + past participle; plural subjects take are, including aren’t in negative statements.", "options": ["bakes", "are baked", "is baked", "baked"], "correct_answer": "are baked"}, "reason": "EXPLANATION_OR_ACCESSIBILITY_CLEANUP"}, {"n": 1181, "original_id": "bc1d6814-5a9e-49a9-9302-c71958a802d2", "original_hash": "c76ccbc31d026581f3ce7a263f790b2aebc4ae66a4370b1c12c9dcf94794fedf", "replacement_id": "c0b16b17-3096-596f-8563-bf736e6848e1", "patch": {"explanation": "The subject receives the action. The present simple passive uses am/is/are + past participle; plural subjects take are, including aren’t in negative statements.", "options": ["is taught", "teaches", "taught", "is teaching"], "correct_answer": "is taught"}, "reason": "EXPLANATION_OR_ACCESSIBILITY_CLEANUP"}, {"n": 1182, "original_id": "f483c852-8aaa-44c4-9867-823610498777", "original_hash": "491b68cd585bd58302b1bb72bacad7c4b76b85e3ed0aec58700ee8a9d8400149", "replacement_id": "5da07819-d07c-56b4-9e2a-9e4f5382d79f", "patch": {"explanation": "The subject receives the action. The present simple passive uses am/is/are + past participle; plural subjects take are, including aren’t in negative statements.", "options": ["isn’t required", "aren’t required", "don’t required", "not required"], "correct_answer": "aren’t required"}, "reason": "EXPLANATION_OR_ACCESSIBILITY_CLEANUP"}, {"n": 1183, "original_id": "2a78a587-088d-4667-9b8a-5b797a52583c", "original_hash": "5e03129792263c083518cb29364b738249032b6b888d3754f74068526d9a1cc5", "replacement_id": "01069319-f77c-554d-9f50-a61154271c5e", "patch": {"question_text": "Choose the list punctuation that uses the serial (Oxford) comma before and.", "explanation": "The requested serial comma appears after milk, before and. Omitting it can be acceptable under a different punctuation style.", "options": ["I bought apples milk and bread.", "I bought apples, milk and bread.", "I bought apples, milk, and bread.", "I, bought apples, milk, and bread."], "correct_answer": "I bought apples, milk, and bread."}, "reason": "OPTIONAL_OXFORD_COMMA"}, {"n": 1203, "original_id": "1d9d31c3-169f-3659-0a8b-3c323aa0070a", "original_hash": "460ecb49880814c1a51a6fb6d2867fe20c84f3bda7e82724ff0addad9010a368", "replacement_id": "db237bcd-4f97-5f64-bccd-4c43da543aa6", "patch": {"question_text": "First read the question. _____ choose the best answer. Finally, submit your answer.", "explanation": "Then marks the middle step; finally already introduces the last step.", "options": ["Then", "Finally", "Because", "But"], "correct_answer": "Then"}, "reason": "SEQUENCE_CONNECTOR_ALTERNATIVE"}, {"n": 1206, "original_id": "44919854-df08-9952-baff-e77b8894f02f", "original_hash": "de9bae4b4f0c70c378c4f35b8c62e13b9266ee4b9e92f4c32b3f3e91060900a8", "replacement_id": "5723c1fb-0d69-59cd-b397-87c2676159f7", "patch": {"question_text": "Choose the usual everyday plural of person when referring to several individuals.", "options": ["people", "person’s", "peoples", "persones"], "explanation": "People is the usual everyday plural. Persons is also grammatical in formal/legal use, so it is not offered as a wrong choice.", "correct_answer": "people"}, "reason": "ACCEPTED_PLURAL_VARIANT"}, {"n": 1213, "original_id": "72ed7e24-760c-e391-8d90-5d2fd2fbe651", "original_hash": "898eed12006b4ff7ab963f480c6841a26066ef3a249ce786d72c388c75cdd7ec", "replacement_id": "4d944d64-b89e-581f-b502-469e976203c7", "patch": {"question_text": "Complete the sentence about yesterday: The students _____ very quiet during the test.", "explanation": "The time is explicitly in the past. Use was with a singular subject and were with a plural subject.", "options": ["were", "was", "are", "is"], "correct_answer": "were"}, "reason": "TENSE_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 1224, "original_id": "d66c7510-8270-01ab-1e7e-84a314c984af", "original_hash": "4828e38238db095dba67a1afdd868822d76cda5fe640bb0bbabe8b7b67358d99", "replacement_id": "0c989c26-0f95-5a56-b650-a2c9c2683e9f", "patch": {"question_text": "Use an indefinite article for an unspecified singular item or person, not something already identified. I need _____ eraser for this exercise.", "explanation": "Use a before a consonant sound and an before a vowel sound. European and university begin with a /j/ consonant sound; hour begins with a vowel sound.", "options": ["an", "a", "the", "no article"], "correct_answer": "an"}, "reason": "ARTICLE_REFERENCE_UNDERSPECIFIED"}, {"n": 1247, "original_id": "0a9d74cf-7dbc-461e-8509-4c479260f543", "original_hash": "5b93b2d3c80d21919f319396fb88ece6d369e6058342fecedf3ff89b6f92df13", "replacement_id": "67ff0a46-c017-5506-8bd4-b20fcf3ab3a6", "patch": {"topic": "Compound Adjectives", "topic_name": "Compound Adjectives", "explanation": "A compound adjective joins words to describe a noun: sky-high describes the building; mouth-watering describes an appetising meal.", "options": ["Sky-High", "Sky-Highed", "Sky-Heighted", "Sky-Hightet"], "correct_answer": "Sky-High"}, "reason": "LEGACY_TOPIC_LABEL_MISMATCH"}, {"n": 1248, "original_id": "327846d9-75b9-48f5-b03b-0af6fad4ad8b", "original_hash": "8ba7b5689e7956df28b1212c2ba1d13f17ab9a0274725735f61d1b40959b2660", "replacement_id": "ab04df77-810e-5255-92b6-e0c62a84899a", "patch": {"topic": "Compound Adjectives", "topic_name": "Compound Adjectives", "explanation": "A compound adjective joins words to describe a noun: sky-high describes the building; mouth-watering describes an appetising meal.", "options": ["Mouth-water", "Mouth-watered", "Mouth-watering", "Mouth-watery"], "correct_answer": "Mouth-watering"}, "reason": "LEGACY_TOPIC_LABEL_MISMATCH"}, {"n": 1249, "original_id": "575fe7ed-3a28-4fd7-b947-18fb3a0e6ed6", "original_hash": "c85df6658e2fbbb8d62e5ab06cd59eaae14b4fc98dc3c8d1c19ffe2ef24273f7", "replacement_id": "ae69b7e3-a6bd-5f25-bb2e-ff03ee34f693", "patch": {"question_text": "Which word is the noun in this sentence? The quick runner crossed the river.", "options": ["crossed", "quick", "river", "the"], "explanation": "River names a thing and functions as a noun in this sentence.", "correct_answer": "river"}, "reason": "WORD_CLASS_REQUIRES_CONTEXT"}, {"n": 1251, "original_id": "d7f5e887-0ed5-4f84-b2f2-f0ae35b3cdce", "original_hash": "c9c233dc4abd6feea53d388e9c2b22b493c706931aa992e9513e652cbb5f511e", "replacement_id": "6e24703f-c332-5b95-91c3-05d1346cf5fa", "patch": {"topic": "Figurative Language", "topic_name": "Figurative Language", "options": ["He ran like the wind.", "Time is a thief.", "She is as brave as a lion.", "They fought like cats and dogs."], "correct_answer": "Time is a thief."}, "reason": "LEGACY_TOPIC_LABEL_MISMATCH"}, {"n": 1252, "original_id": "e60091e9-6b63-4a92-b3fa-f12e34ed1db0", "original_hash": "5db4fabc9b4d4a700b108e40312b46c7413440d432aff7ef49b94bad9f2eb1c3", "replacement_id": "413a626d-4ba8-5a17-b902-7f29816c6be7", "patch": {"topic": "Compound Sentences", "topic_name": "Compound Sentences", "options": ["I studied.", "Studying hard.", "I studied, and I slept.", "Because I studied."], "correct_answer": "I studied, and I slept."}, "reason": "LEGACY_TOPIC_LABEL_MISMATCH"}, {"n": 1254, "original_id": "69642a4b-57b2-4672-b4c1-a66bfac9a1f2", "original_hash": "5a17ead1c5ae9b290d098b51f6c38901289741eb93a98f20a9f9ee04e7e469d0", "replacement_id": "3c9d5afe-152c-5179-aa0d-78c3cd779e10", "patch": {"options": ["who", "which", "where", "whom"], "explanation": "Who is the subject of lives and refers to the teacher. That can also be valid and is therefore removed as a distractor.", "correct_answer": "who"}, "reason": "ACCEPTED_RELATIVE_VARIANT"}, {"n": 1255, "original_id": "84a8116f-aedc-4805-9c47-3486efba5aff", "original_hash": "19a1caf717537d484aeaea4af12460915a65fef234545487bcfcc87957a5d370", "replacement_id": "4329c471-f0cc-51b9-b842-09f1f1787bae", "patch": {"question_text": "Use the formal object relative pronoun for a person: The woman ___ we met yesterday is my neighbour.", "explanation": "Whom is the formal object relative pronoun. Who is also accepted in ordinary use, but this task explicitly asks for the formal object form.", "options": ["who", "whom", "which", "where"], "correct_answer": "whom"}, "reason": "ACCEPTED_RELATIVE_VARIANT"}, {"n": 1265, "original_id": "2a656081-533d-4b1a-8c14